% ============================================================================
% element_category_property — the three BROAD categories of chemical element
% and the defining electrical property the source states for each
% (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY).
% ============================================================================
% Before the periodic table sorts elements into columns (see element-groups,
% which maps a SPECIFIC element to its group family), it first sorts every
% element into just THREE broad categories by how the element behaves —
% especially whether it conducts electricity. "Metals are the good conductors;
% nonmetals are the poor conductors; metalloids are the in-between ones we call
% semiconductors." This is one of the very first sorting ideas a chemistry
% student learns, and a rung a CHEMISTRY agent reasons UP from: to reason about
% an unfamiliar element you first decide WHICH of the three categories it is in.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? element_category_property(metal, $Property)      % good_conductor
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `element_category_property(category, property)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the property WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these three broad categories (it never
% invents a property).
%
% The three broad categories and the defining electrical property the source
% states for each, as a little truth table:
%
%     category    defining electrical property   the source's cue
%     ---------   ----------------------------   ------------------------------
%     metal       good_conductor                 "good conductors ... free electrons"
%     nonmetal    poor_conductor                 "poor conductors of heat and electricity"
%     metalloid   semiconductor                  "act as electrical conductors ...
%                                                  thus they are called semiconductors"
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a property and recall its category:
%
%     ? element_category_property($Category, poor_conductor)   % nonmetal
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three broad categories — `compound`, `alloy`,
% `mixture` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a category recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a property. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% category→property pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `property` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% source's OWN word for the category's electrical behaviour — metals are "good
% conductors", nonmetals are "poor conductors", and metalloids are called
% "semiconductors" — so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted
% span. (These three atoms are also pairwise distinct, so the reverse binding
% query is unambiguous.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every category→property
% pair below was read out of a fetched Chemistry LibreTexts page this session,
% and any pair whose property could not be verified there would have been
% dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the
% verbatim span that states it:
%
%   metal → good_conductor
%     https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map:_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/07:_Periodic_Properties_of_the_Elements/7.06:_Metals_Nonmetals_and_Metalloids
%     (Chemistry LibreTexts — "Chemistry: The Central Science" (Brown et al.) map)
%     "Metals are good conductors because they have free electrons."
%
%   nonmetal → poor_conductor
%     (same LibreTexts page, Nonmetals section)
%     "They are non-lustrous, brittle and poor conductors of heat and
%      electricity (except graphite)."
%
%   metalloid → semiconductor
%     (same LibreTexts page, Metalloids section)
%     "Some metalloids, such as silicon and germanium, can act as electrical
%      conductors under the right conditions, thus they are called
%      semiconductors."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the LibreTexts statement that
% fixes the first row (metal → good_conductor). Every OTHER row's per-fact
% source and verbatim span is listed above, so each property remains
% independently checkable. The source is Chemistry LibreTexts, a widely used
% open teaching resource (a course-textbook summary, not a primary standards
% body), so `trust consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table element_category_property {
    columns category, property

    row (metal, good_conductor)
    row (nonmetal, poor_conductor)
    row (metalloid, semiconductor)

    source "Metals are good conductors because they have free electrons."
    locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Map:_Chemistry_-_The_Central_Science_(Brown_et_al.)/07:_Periodic_Properties_of_the_Elements/7.06:_Metals_Nonmetals_and_Metalloids"
    trust consensus
}
